WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:07.480
Just hear me out.

00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:09.060
It's 1986.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:13.760
Oprah is interviewing sax player Kenneth Gorelick
-- known around the world as Kenny G.

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:19.020
"It's like you are talking to, and doing a few other things with this instrument."

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:23.180
"Well I've never taken any music lessons so — " "Amazing!" " — I don't really know exactly what I'm doing."

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:26.900
In 1993 he played at Bill Clinton’s inauguration.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.820
By 1996 he was starring in golf commercials.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:37.180
"My driver use to be my least-favorite club
in the bag, until I got the Great Big Bertha driver."

00:00:37.180 --> 00:00:38.420
"Now it's my favorite!"

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:41.020
And his music was the calming soundtrack of

00:00:41.020 --> 00:00:42.200
the weather channel.

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:48.340
Kenny G had hit his peak.

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.920
“Tell me that part about Kenny G again”

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:57.900
But it wasn’t just Kenny G; smooth jazz,
the style of music he’d come to be the face

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:59.860
of, was everywhere.

00:01:00.660 --> 00:01:02.800
"Smooth jazz, 94.7"

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:04.380
"Smooth jazz, 106.5"

00:01:04.380 --> 00:01:06.240
"Smooth jazz, 98.7"

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:09.220
"This is smooth jazz, 106.9"

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:10.760
How did that happen?

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.020
Like, where the hell did smooth jazz come from?

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.220
Let’s go back to Clinton’s inauguration.

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:29.160
Kenny G actually wasn’t the only saxophonist performing.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:30.440
In fact, Will Smith,

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:31.560
"This feels great."

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.480
The emcee of one of many
inaugural events that day, brought out 10

00:01:36.480 --> 00:01:39.580
sax players to serenade the jazz-loving president.

00:01:41.020 --> 00:01:42.000
"Check this out."

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:45.340
This was like the who’s who of saxophonists,

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:51.060
but it’s this guy, standing right next to

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.120
Kenny G, where this story begins.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:02:00.520
There's no question: Kenny G has one idol and
he admits to it.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.260
Grover Washington Jr.

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:10.680
In the late '60s and early '70s, straight jazz
was all but gone from pop radio.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:15.460
The music was becoming more experimental,
and albums like Miles Davis’ “B*tches Brew"

00:02:15.460 --> 00:02:21.180
established jazz fusion - an eclectic
hybrid of jazz and rock - as the next iteration

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:22.820
of the art form.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:28.020
But some jazz artists saw promise in pop music,
and began crossing over by recording instrumental

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:30.160
covers of huge pop hits.

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.620
A shining example of that is jazz guitar virtuoso,
Wes Montgomery’s,

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:37.840
1966 cover of “Goin' Out of My Head."

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:05.060
That record was produced by Creed Taylor,
who filled out Montgomery’s mellow guitar

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:10.140
with strings and woodwinds - instruments that
were more familiar to pop radio listeners.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:14.480
This song sounded more like The Beach Boys’
"Pet Sounds" than it did most jazz records that

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:16.860
came out in 1966.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:22.380
And it was a smash hit, because it established a formula
for jazz that the everyday listener could

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:23.280
understand.

00:03:23.580 --> 00:03:29.300
As this Billboard article put it, "The chasm
between jazz and popular music was narrowing."

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:38.400
It was through Creed Taylor’s label, CTI,
that Grover Washington Jr. became a household

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:39.200
name.

00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:43.880
Grover Washington defined crossover jazz in
the '70s.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:46.960
His fourth album with Taylor was "Mister Magic."

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:50.720
Just looking at the cover, you know you’re
going to listen to songs that are smooth as ****.

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:55.120
It really is, in the most simple sense an R&amp;B

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:58.060
background, of a fairly slow tempo

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:02.240
with fairly slow harmonic change that's kind of grooving.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:07.240
On top of that is Grover’s saxophone playing
a simple, yet infectious melody.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:19.800
The second he made radio-friendly songs, he
lost a lot of respect from the jazz world,

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.260
and it was difficult gaining it back.

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:26.780
Here’s a quote on the back cover of his
own record that reveals this tension:

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.960
Apparently a jazz critic went to one of his shows and

00:04:29.960 --> 00:04:32.580
sat scowling at the bar. By the end of the show,

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.140
he said, "Cat can play."

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:38.020
Just having that jazz critic admit that he could play the saxophone,

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:41.280
was equivalent to a bushel of five-star reviews.

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:45.640
Any jazz player will give you a list of things that
they don't like about it.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:48.180
They don't like the lack of technical virtuosity.

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:55.600
They don't like the lack of harmonic interest,
but in my opinion what trumps it all is popularity.

00:04:55.600 --> 00:05:00.920
When you're dealing with art cliques, popularity
is like poison.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:05.080
If jazz purists were quick to dismiss Grover's
"Mister Magic," then they sure as hell were

00:05:05.080 --> 00:05:10.460
going to roll their eyes at jazz guitarist,
George Benson's, "Breezin' " in 1976.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.320
It was the first jazz album to ever go platinum.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:24.180
The title track was originally composed by
soul record producer Bobby Womack and bears

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:27.500
a strikingly close resemblance to “Goin'
Out of My Head.”

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:44.660
George Benson is one artist that no one could
refute, because he had the chops in

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:45.860
straight-ahead jazz.

00:05:45.860 --> 00:05:49.020
And people were mad in the straight-ahead
jazz industry.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:51.620
To them, he chose success over art.

00:05:51.620 --> 00:05:54.680
This Downbeat magazine review of George Benson
says it all.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:59.240
“Hearing George Benson on this album is
like watching Marlon Brando in a Three Stooges

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:03.160
movie - such is the relationship between the
art and artist.”

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:06.020
But, that didn't really matter to the public.

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:11.580
"This Masquerade," the single off the record,
peaked at number 10 on the Billboard Hot 100,

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:15.520
and won the Grammy in 1977 for Record of the Year.

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:25.180
He does this scat-type singing, where he’s
scatting and he's playing and the fingers are

00:06:25.180 --> 00:06:26.660
following his voice.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:35.920
And you couldn't say that wasn't jazz in 1976.

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:41.340
By the end of the '70s, dozens of musicians followed Benson and Grover’s breezy sound.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:05.260
Even Taxi, one of the most popular shows to
ever to be on television, used a Bob James

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:08.040
pop-jazz recording as their theme song.

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:14.020
The problem?

00:07:14.020 --> 00:07:16.260
Well, radio didn't know what to call it.

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:19.900
This reporter just said “Not Quite Jazz,
But Pretty Stuff.”

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:25.760
Enter Broadcast Architecture, a market research
firm tasked with giving this promising radio

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:26.960
format a name.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.400
"All these radio stations were coming on,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:31.160
doing this format."

00:07:31.560 --> 00:07:33.280
"It was like, what do we call it?"

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:38.080
"The way we did focus groups is a little different;
we would interview people one person at a

00:07:38.080 --> 00:07:39.700
time for 30 minutes."

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:41.820
A female radio listener entered the room.

00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:43.580
"She was saying

00:07:43.580 --> 00:07:45.960
'it’s jazz, but it’s not really jazz and it’s smooth.'

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:48.480
Then she goes, 'It’s smooth jazz.' "

00:07:48.600 --> 00:07:49.700
"Yeah, that’s what it is."

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:54.380
"It really struck a lightbulb with
everybody that was behind the glass watching."

00:07:54.540 --> 00:07:56.220
Turn it on 94.7 - okay.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:57.100
94.7.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:07:58.240
"Ninety - four - seven."

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:01.700
That’s it that’s it, sh sh sh sh shhh!

00:08:01.700 --> 00:08:07.400
"On behalf of all of us at Metropolitan Broadcasting,
welcome to 94.7, The Wave."

00:08:11.660 --> 00:08:17.380
What you just heard was the moment one of the first
official smooth jazz stations went on the air.

00:08:17.380 --> 00:08:21.220
"Within a few months KKSF in San Francisco
launched.

00:08:21.240 --> 00:08:22.960
WNUA launched.

00:08:23.300 --> 00:08:25.000
CD101.9 in New York launched."

00:08:25.340 --> 00:08:28.240
"It was a tipping point in the format
for sure."

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:33.520
But take a look at how The Wave marketed itself
- only occasionally did they actually play

00:08:33.520 --> 00:08:35.940
what some would consider jazz music.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:40.520
"We started testing everything from Phil Collins...

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:45.360
Even some Hall and Oates tunes."

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:48.920
"You know, vocal tracks that would help glue
it all together."

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:52.380
"Smooth sounds for a rough world."

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:57.460
And make no mistake, Kenny G was at the center
of all of it.

00:08:57.460 --> 00:09:02.300
"CD101.9, it's called "Silhouette" and
the artist, of course, Kenny G."

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:03.160
"Kenny G?"

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:03.860
"Who likes jazz?"

00:09:03.860 --> 00:09:04.660
I love jazz.

00:09:04.660 --> 00:09:06.400
"Kenny G can blow the storm up."

00:09:06.400 --> 00:09:08.820
"Ladies and gentleman, Kenny G!"

00:09:08.820 --> 00:09:13.280
Kenny G was known just as much for his hair
as he was for his saxophone playing.

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:18.140
"He was the cool white boy.
He was just a cool guy who played the saxophone."

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:21.420
"And I'm going to go ahead and say it, it's the money."

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:24.520
"He made so much money doing it."

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:27.760
"And this year's adult contemporary artist
is…

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:28.920
Kenny G!"

00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:32.880
"I don't know what to say, I would have never
expected I’d win this thing."

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:34.500
Yes you did, Kenny.

00:09:34.500 --> 00:09:40.640
This chart shows the rise of smooth jazz radio
starting in 1987 when KTWV went on the air

00:09:40.640 --> 00:09:45.860
and peaked around 1997, the same year Kenny
G entered the Guinness world records books

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:49.160
for holding a note for over 45 minutes…..
wait what?!

00:09:56.140 --> 00:10:00.900
Some of smooth jazz’s most attentive fans
were the at-work radio listeners of corporate

00:10:00.900 --> 00:10:01.560
America.

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:05.580
"If you take The Wave to work with you and there's a fax machine in your office, jot down a few

00:10:05.580 --> 00:10:08.660
songs that you'd like to hear on The Wave and fax them to us."

00:10:08.660 --> 00:10:14.020
"We would get hundreds and hundreds of faxes, like within an hour the fax machine starts rolling."

00:10:16.020 --> 00:10:18.680
Smooth jazz seemed like it would dominate forever.

00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:26.500
But then, everything changed.

00:10:28.280 --> 00:10:34.200
In the early 2000's Arbitron, the firm that
measures audiences, introduced a new technology,

00:10:34.400 --> 00:10:35.300
The Purple People Eater —

00:10:35.300 --> 00:10:38.100
I’m sorry I meant to say the "Portable People Meter."

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:40.980
It's this little beeper -- people believe
it killed smooth jazz.

00:10:40.980 --> 00:10:46.680
PPM, which is still in use today - is an electronic
beeper that captures audio tones masked in

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:52.560
the signal of radio broadcasts. Basically, it picks up
audience listenership automatically.

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:57.720
It replaced a decades-long practice of using paper diary entries to measure audiences.

00:10:57.720 --> 00:11:02.580
"People would write down for a week what they listened to and they would turn it in. Very easy for people to do."

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:05.660
"It went from that to,

00:11:05.660 --> 00:11:11.020
what we want to ask you to do is wear this on your belt all day

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:14.000
and we want you to do this for a year."

00:11:14.000 --> 00:11:16.660
But it often didn't work with smooth jazz.

00:11:16.660 --> 00:11:21.040
The format’s soft, ambient sound didn’t
allow for the signal to be consistently masked

00:11:21.040 --> 00:11:26.020
in the music without being discernable to
listeners - if the signal wasn’t embedded,

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:28.240
the beeper just couldn’t register it.

00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:33.980
Polling site Fivethirtyeight tracked the
number of six large-market smooth jazz stations

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:40.280
before and after PPM - in each instance they
either changed formats or shutdown entirely.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:43.460
But it might not have been all PPM’s fault

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:47.360
"I think it’s a reflection of what our economy did.

00:11:47.360 --> 00:11:50.220
Our station went off the air when everything crashed."

00:11:50.840 --> 00:11:56.720
Smooth jazz radio was music for ordinary,
everyday people trying to get through their

00:11:56.720 --> 00:11:57.980
day stress-free.

00:11:58.700 --> 00:12:03.040
It certainly never cared about critics
during its solid 20 year run, and unlike

00:12:03.040 --> 00:12:07.080
straight-ahead jazz, it didn’t care so
much about challenging the listener either.

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:10.000
And it’s why from the 1960s to the '90s

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:12.980
anything written about the music looked like this:

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:20.140
But dig deep into smooth jazz’s history
and you’ll find some really exciting music.

00:12:20.140 --> 00:12:23.200
"There was an album Herbie Hancock did call
the "New Standard."

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:25.460
"Oh man that was good.

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:27.000
I'd come off there talking about that."

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:30.280
"I was like, Oh this is what this is why I'm
doing what I'm doing."

00:12:30.280 --> 00:12:34.120
Or go even further back to Grover Washington
Jr.’s "Winelight."

00:12:34.120 --> 00:12:37.080
"And just listen to it as you're cooking dinner or something."

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:42.960
"It's just chill, man. And it'll give you a feeling for why people fell in love with this music.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:45.100
For such a long time."

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:51.120
Thanks so much for watching my little miniseries on jazz,

00:12:51.120 --> 00:12:55.680
I hope to tackle so many more stories on this genre of music in future Earworm episodes.

00:12:56.480 --> 00:12:58.500
Until then, I've got a great gift for you,

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:04.640
which is a Spotify playlist full of amazing smooth jazz songs that will definitely make you a convert.

